insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 1, 'On the published linear_star the closed form and Newton both return w1 = 546.666666667 psia, a difference of 0.0000e+0 psia. What has that agreement established?', '["That the assembly, the signs and the boundary handling are right, because Gaussian elimination and Newton share no code and no reasoning at all.", "That the Newton tolerance was tight enough, since a looser stopping criterion would have left the wellhead short of the closed form value.", "That the branch relation is differentiable at every pressure difference, which is the precondition a matrix inverse places on a resistor.", "That the independent bisection referee has confirmed the linear case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two routes with nothing in common landing on the same number is evidence about the model rather than about either method, and at h the difference is -2.8422e-14 psia, relative 1.1269e-16.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 2, 'Newton converges on linear_star in 2 iterations and the gate asserts three iterations or fewer. Why is that assertion worth making?', '["The line search halves every correction on a linear system, so each extra halving shows up as one extra iteration and the count tracks the halvings.", "A four node network is small, and the iteration count on any network scales with the number of unknowns the solver has to carry.", "Newton is exact on a linear system, so needing many steps would mean the Jacobian is wrong rather than that the problem is hard.", "The tolerance of 1e-12 is loose enough on a linear case that the first correction already lands inside it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reported residual on that run is 7.2760e-12 lb/d with nothing pinned, and the step count is a statement about the derivative rather than about the difficulty.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 3, 'A caller hands solveNetwork a network whose branches are all linear. What does the module do about the closed form that exists for it?', '["It detects the linear branch relations and dispatches to solveLinearNetwork, which is why the run comes back in 2 iterations.", "Nothing. Nothing selects solveLinearNetwork for the caller and nothing warns that a linear case has reached the iterative path.", "It runs Newton and then repeats the case through the closed form, reporting the difference between the two as its own check on the assembly.", "buildNetwork refuses a network that mixes linear and turbulent branches, so the closed form is always available wherever it applies."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The closed form is exercised only by the JS gate on a three node star, and the independent referee has no linear method of its own: it sweeps that star 19 times like everything else.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 4, 'Two linear branches of 200 and 300 lb/d per psi in series give a wellhead of 183.333333333 psia, matched exactly by one branch of 120.000000000 lb/d per psi. What happens to that rule on a gathering system of turbulent branches?', '["It survives for series branches and fails only for parallel ones, because a square root law still adds its drops end to end along a single path, and only a junction breaks that addition.", "It survives once each conductance is squared first, which converts lb/d per root psi into the linear units the rule needs.", "It survives, since the module normalises every branch relation before assembly.", "It does not survive. A turbulent conductance is quoted in lb/d per root psi, which is a different quantity, and the reciprocal rule is a fact about resistances."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same two branches in parallel behave as one of 500 lb/d per psi at 120.000000000 psia, and neither identity has any meaning once the branch law is a square root.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 5, 'buildNetwork returns 8 nodes, 8 branches, 7 unknown pressures and 1 delivery point on the teaching network AGBADA WEST. Why is the separator not among the unknowns?', '["A sink has a fixed pressure and takes whatever arrives, so it is the boundary the whole system is solved against rather than a pressure to be found.", "It carries only 1 branch, and a node with a single branch on it has no mass balance to write.", "Its pressure is recovered afterwards from the trunk flow, so it is solved but not counted among the unknowns the solver indexes.", "The module fixes it at MIN_PRESSURE_PSIA = 14.7 psia and refuses to move it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The unknowns are indexed t1, t2, t3, t4, ha, hb and hc, and every one of them has to be anchored to something the caller fixed or the mass balance has nothing to find.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 6, 'The teaching network reports residualLbD = 1.546141e-11 lb/d, and its nodal imbalance table carries 3.450000e+2 lb/d at t4. What does the reported number describe?', '["The worst imbalance anywhere in the network, so the 3.450000e+2 lb/d at t4 has to be a rounding artefact of the imbalance table, printed at a scale the maximum itself never used.", "The worst imbalance over the nodes the iteration was still moving, which on this answer is the -1.546141e-11 lb/d at ha and not the largest imbalance in the network.", "The total mass error summed over the seven unknown nodes, which is why it is so much smaller than any single entry.", "The distance the pressures moved on the last accepted step, in lb/d after the Jacobian has converted it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'checkConservation on the same answer gives produced = 13300.677150912 lb/d against delivered = 12955.677150912 lb/d, a gap of 345.000000000 lb/d, and the residual never saw it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 7, 'A network answer is quoted with converged = true and a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d and nothing else. What is missing?', '["The oracle comparison, which the module runs alongside every solve with a bracketed bisection at each node, and which it returns as a second and independent residual.", "The iteration count, which is the only field in the return that can be compared against a published case.", "The conservation audit on the same answer, because solveNetwork never calls checkConservation and nothing else in the return came from anywhere but the iteration.", "Nothing is missing. A residual near machine precision is what a checked answer looks like."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The audit reports a gap of 345.000000000 lb/d, 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says was produced, on the answer whose flag says converged.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 8, 'The Jacobian is built by moving one node pressure by max(1e-3, |p| * 1e-5), about 0.007805 psi at the teaching network junction pressures, and re-evaluating everything. What does that buy?', '["An exact derivative of the branch law, since the module differentiates the turbulent relation analytically once the step size is chosen.", "A cheaper step than an analytic Jacobian, because one residual evaluation covers every column at once and no relation is re-entered.", "A guarantee that no step can drive a node below MIN_PRESSURE_PSIA = 14.7 psia, which is what the step size is sized to prevent.", "A derivative that assumes nothing about the branch law beyond the contract, at the price of a full residual evaluation for every column."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is expensive per step and cheap in steps: 2 iterations on linear_star, 6 on turbulent_tree against 48 sweeps for the bisection referee, and 11 on the teaching network.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 9, 'A full Newton correction on a network with a nearly dead leg can drive a node below atmospheric. What does the module do instead?', '["It rejects the branch that caused the overshoot and re-solves the network without it, reporting that branch as a dead leg and leaving its pressure where the last accepted step had put it.", "It halves the step until the residual actually improves, trying thirty halvings before it gives up, and holds every pressure at or above 14.7 psia.", "It widens the Jacobian step on the offending column until the differenced entry stops changing sign across zero, which is what turns a divergence into a solve.", "It clamps the correction to the pressure of the nearest delivery point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A published fixture runs a weak well against a 20 psia separator through a very slack line and converges at a wellhead of 20.003996803 psia and a rate of 19.984012790 lb/d with nothing pinned.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 10, 'Solved alone on their own flowlines, three teaching wells take 7 iterations each and the fourth takes 4 with a reported residual of exactly 0.0000e+0 lb/d. Which run is the one to distrust?', '["The 4 iteration run, whose conservation gap is 3.450000e+2 lb/d, relative 3.502538e-1.", "None of them, because all four report converged = true and the fastest run met the same tolerance in fewer steps.", "The 7 iteration runs, since a longer path means the line search was rejecting steps and the answer sits where the halving stopped.", "The run whose reported residual is 9.0949e-13 lb/d rather than zero, because a non-zero residual is an unconverged one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fewer steps meant fewer equations rather than a better answer, and the other three gaps are 9.094947e-13, -4.547474e-13 and 0.000000e+0 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 11, 'On the published turbulent_tree the trunk b5 carries 8606.086171612 lb/d and the three well rates are 2853.835862213, 1770.680431189 and 3981.569878309 lb/d. Why do those tie out branch by branch?', '["The trunk conductance of 410 lb/d per root psi was chosen to pass the sum, and the solver moves the two headers until the branch carries exactly that.", "propagateStreams reconciles the branch masses against the well rates before the flows are returned, so a tree is the case where that reconciliation has nothing left to do.", "There is exactly one path from each well to the separator, so every branch flow is settled by the well rates downstream of it.", "A turbulent branch conserves mass only in a tree, since mass in equals mass out is asserted along a whole path rather than across each branch on it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flows come first and pressures follow in a tree, and the engine lands within 7.5975e-9 lb/d of the bisection referee on b1.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 12, 'A study proposes to compare a trunk quoted at 720 lb/d per root psi with a linear branch quoted at 400 lb/d per psi and pick the tighter one. What is wrong with the comparison?', '["Nothing is wrong with the units, but the comparison needs the bore of each branch before the two conductances can be ranked against each other.", "The turbulent number has to be squared first, after which the two are in the same units and rank in the obvious way.", "The comparison holds only while both branches carry the same mass.", "They are not the same quantity. One multiplies a square root of a pressure difference and the other multiplies the difference itself, so the two numbers never compare."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Doubling a turbulent conductance does not halve a drop, and the reciprocal rule that collapses a 200 and a 300 lb/d per psi pair into 120.000000000 lb/d per psi has no counterpart at all.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 13, 'The engine and the independent bisection referee both report that they converged on turbulent_tree. What are the two claims about?', '["Different quantities. The engine stops on a nodal mass imbalance in lb/d and the referee stops on how far the pressures moved between sweeps, in psia.", "The same quantity measured twice, which is why the counts differ: 6 Newton iterations against 48 sweeps on the same case.", "The same quantity, since the goldens publish both criteria so the two can be set side by side.", "The pressure agreement between them, which is what each of the two flags is tested against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither criterion is published in the golden files, so a case both methods agree on says nothing about how either of them decided to stop.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 14, 'The gate holds turbulent_tree pressures to 5 decimals and flows to 4, looped pressures to 5 decimals, and the wells_fight header and rates to 4 decimals. What follows for anything smaller than those bands?', '["It is caught anyway, because the golden files carry the full double precision values the referee produced and the bands only round the report.", "It is invisible to the published comparison by construction, so an agreement inside a band is not evidence that nothing differs there.", "It is caught by checkConservation, which is run on every golden case and holds the mass to machine precision rather than to a decimal count.", "It is impossible, since two independent methods cannot differ below the last published decimal."]'::jsonb, 1, 'linear_star is the exception in kind rather than in size: it is held to a relative 1e-12 against a closed form, which is the only check with no tolerance in it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-solve', 15, 'Which condition makes solveNetwork return ok = false, as against a warning?', '["A conservation gap larger than a documented fraction of what the wells were credited with, which is the one audit the solver runs on itself.", "A node whose pressure is left where it sits because nothing depended on it, since an unsolved node cannot be part of a solved answer.", "A singular Jacobian, where two or more nodes move together so their pressures are not separately determined.", "An iteration that ran its cap without meeting its tolerance, because a cap that is reached is a failure to solve."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The other three come back as warnings the caller has to read, and underneath the singular case solveLinear returns null rather than an array of infinities, and it pivots.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 1, 'In the published tree the trunk carries 8606.086171631 lb/d, exactly its three well rates added. What does the published case looped take away from that ordering?', '["Nothing about the ordering, since the loop only adds a second delivery route and the well rates still fix every branch downstream of them.", "The ability to compute any branch first, because each leg flow is set by the header and midpoint pressures and those pressures are set by how the flow split.", "The mass balance at the midpoint node, which a loop leaves underdetermined until the caller supplies a split fraction of its own.", "The conservation check, which cannot be run on a network carrying a loop."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In looped the two legs carry 3693.942751108 and 3640.772530876 lb/d, and neither number exists until the pressures h = 621.148550879 psia and m = 473.868277305 psia do.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 2, 'On looped the direct leg is quoted at 180 lb/d per root psi and the midpoint leg at 220, yet the direct leg takes 50.362456 percent of the delivery. Why?', '["The direct leg is the shorter of the two, and a shorter branch of the same conductance carries more because the drop is spread over less pipe, which is what a conductance is quoted per.", "The two legs do not start at the same node, so their conductances were never comparable in the first place and the share follows the wells.", "The 220 leg runs backwards over part of the sweep, which is what takes its share below half.", "The midpoint route is the feed at 300 lb/d per root psi and then the 220 leg in series, so it spends two pressure drops against the direct leg''s one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Turbulent conductance has no reciprocal rule, so no single number collapses the 300 and the 220 into something worth comparing with 180.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 3, 'What condition does the flow split at a loop satisfy at the solution?', '["Both routes leave the same node and arrive at the same node, so both must spend the same total pressure, and the split is what makes that equality hold.", "Each route carries mass in proportion to its own conductance, which is what the module means by sharing a loop.", "Each route carries the same mass, since a loop with two paths is symmetric once the pressures have settled.", "The route with the lower total conductance carries whatever the other route cannot."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The direct leg spends 621.148550879 psia down to 200.000000000 psia in one drop and the other route spends the same header down through 473.868277305 psia in two.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 4, 'On looped the feed b3 carries 3640.772530877 lb/d and the midpoint leg b5 carries 3640.772530876 lb/d. What does that near identity say about the two?', '["They are the two halves of the loop, so the equality between them is the loop closure condition that the solver drives to zero alongside the nodal residuals.", "The midpoint node is pinned at the pressure the initial guess gave it, which is why no mass appears or disappears anywhere across it and the two flows print alike.", "Nothing joins between them, so they are in series and the midpoint route is two resistances rather than the one its own leg conductance suggests.", "Both are at the same conductance, since equal flows in a turbulent branch mean equal conductances."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The route is 300 lb/d per root psi and then 220 lb/d per root psi, and it is the tight part of that pair that decides what raising the walked leg is worth.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 5, 'A sweep on the published looped inputs sets the midpoint leg to 60 lb/d per root psi and the direct leg comes back at 4926.564514 lb/d against 3693.942751 at the published 220. Nothing was done to the direct leg. What happened?', '["The direct leg conductance is recomputed by the solver from the loop total, so changing one leg rescales the other before the solve begins.", "A loop leg is not a private pipe: closing one route moves the header and pushes what it used to carry along the other.", "The wells made more, and the extra mass went to the direct leg because it was the only branch with capacity left.", "The direct leg was carrying part of the midpoint leg''s mass in the published case and gave it back."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The header moves with it, from 949.106108 psia at 60 lb/d per root psi to 621.148551 psia at the published 220, and every well on the system feels that.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 6, 'Walking the midpoint leg from 60 to 100 lb/d per root psi moves its share from 24.634111 to 34.514100 percent, and walking it from 500 to 800 moves the share from 58.833419 to 60.945870 percent. What has flattened?', '["The walked leg has stopped being the tight part of its own route, so more conductance on it buys almost nothing while the feed at 300 lb/d per root psi is untouched.", "The wells, which have reached the flat part of their own inflow curves and can deliver no more mass whatever the loop downstream of them is opened to, so the share stops moving with them.", "The trunk, which has become the branch limiting the whole system once the loop was opened up, and which is not itself on the sweep, so nothing the walked leg does can move it.", "The separator boundary, which was walked down at the same time and has stopped falling."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Delivered follows the same shape, 6536.862501 to 6856.299293 lb/d over the first step and 7602.552441 to 7659.969113 lb/d over the last.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 7, 'The crosslink on AGBADA WEST is drawn from the north manifold to the loop tee and the solve returns its flow as -589.864625170 lb/d. What is the branch doing?', '["Oscillating between the two directions, which is what a negative flow records on a branch the iteration could not settle.", "Carrying 589.864625170 lb/d from the manifold to the tee, with the sign recording that the branch was entered second in the branch list.", "Carrying 589.864625170 lb/d from the loop tee into the north manifold, against the way it was drawn.", "Carrying nothing, since a negative flow on a gathering system is how a dead leg is reported."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flows are signed from the drawn from end to the drawn to end, and the pressure across it is -1.193210823 psi in that sense, so the drawn downstream end is the higher of the two.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 8, 'Why does the crosslink on the teaching network run against its arrow?', '["The trunk takes its mass from the loop tee first, which pulls the tee below the manifold and reverses the branch between them.", "The crosslink conductance of 540 lb/d per root psi is larger than the loop leg at 245, and the larger of two branches on a loop always fills from the other.", "One of the wells on the manifold is capped, so the manifold cannot supply the crosslink and draws through it instead.", "The loop tee is fed by one well through a stiff flowline at 198 lb/d per root psi while the manifold is fed by three through slacker ones, so the tee sits above the manifold."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At the solution ha is 780.469728020 psia and hb is 781.662938843 psia, and the drawing was a guess made before anybody solved anything.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 9, 'Walking the crosslink conductance from 60 to 1400 lb/d per root psi returns flows of -346.749154, -510.593269, -571.240028, -589.864625, -595.655783 and -597.618496 lb/d. What does that show?', '["That the branch is approaching a limit set by its own conductance, beyond which it would run the drawn way.", "That the sweep was run on a fixed pressure difference, which is why only the magnitude moves.", "The sign belongs to the solution rather than to the branch, and no size of pipe reverses it.", "That the module reports the magnitude with a minus sign whenever the branch closes a loop, so the sign is a label rather than a direction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The drawn pressure difference shrinks from -33.398604 psi to -0.182218 psi as the two ends close on each other while the mass rises.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 10, 'What in the returned result names the crosslink reversal?', '["The backflows list from diagnose, which reports the crosslink at -589.864625 lb/d, and dead legs = none beside it.", "A warning from solveNetwork, raised whenever a solved flow direction disagrees with the direction its branch was drawn in.", "A refusal from buildNetwork, which rejects any branch drawn against the pressure gradient that the solve later finds along it.", "Nothing at all, since the minus sign on the flow is the only record of the reversal anywhere in the returned object."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing refuses and nothing warns, so the reversal reaches a reader only as a minus sign on a flow of 589.864625170 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 11, 'buildNetwork accepts the teaching network with 8 nodes, 8 branches, 7 unknown pressures and 1 delivery point, and counts 5 branches on the north manifold. Which of those numbers predicts the crosslink direction?', '["The count of 5 branches on the manifold, since a node with more branches on it is the node the others drain toward.", "None of them. The topology fixes the index the solver works in and settles nothing about which way any branch runs.", "The count of 7 unknown pressures, because the sign of a branch is decided by the order the unknowns are indexed in.", "The single delivery point, which fixes the direction of every branch on the only path to it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every row of the crosslink sweep is negative on the same drawing, from -346.749154 lb/d at 60 lb/d per root psi to -597.618496 at 1400.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 12, 'A drawing has a manifold with no route to any delivery point. What does buildNetwork do?', '["It solves the rest of the network and returns the stranded node with its pressure left wherever the initial guess happened to put it, listed among the pinned nodes.", "It pins the stranded node and warns about it, since a node that nothing else depends upon is exactly the case the pinning path was written for, and carries on with the solve.", "It attaches the node to the nearest delivery point and warns that the topology was repaired.", "It refuses, saying that nothing sets that node''s pressure so the network cannot be solved, and that the node should be connected or taken out."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every failure there is a refusal with a reason rather than a repair, because solving such a drawing would produce a confident answer about a system that does not exist.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 13, 'buildNetwork accepts a loop in a drawing without comment. Which loop does the module refuse?', '["A loop whose two routes carry the same conductance, since the split of mass between them is then undetermined and the Jacobian goes singular on it.", "A loop that carries a backflow, because the drawn direction and the solved direction then close a cycle between the same pair of junctions.", "A loop in the solved flow directions, which propagateStreams rejects as a recirculating network and blames on a branch connected backwards.", "A loop that contains more than one junction, which is refused as a topology the module has no way to index the unknown pressures of."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A gathering system does not recirculate, so the refusal is about the answer rather than about the drawing, and the drawn loop is what makes a network a network.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 14, 'A topology is accepted and the solve still declines with a message about two or more nodes moving together. What is that message about?', '["A singular Jacobian, where the pressures are not separately determined, usually because a branch is connected differently from the way the drawing suggests.", "A loop whose split the module could not resolve, which is the one topology Newton cannot carry.", "Two nodes at the same solved pressure, which leaves the branch between them carrying nothing.", "A node with no route to a delivery point, restated by the solver."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A valid drawing is a precondition for a solve and never evidence of one, and this is the only condition solveNetwork returns ok = false for.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-loops-and-directions', 15, 'The teaching topology is accepted, the solve reports converged = true at a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d, and the audit reports produced = 13300.677150912 lb/d against delivered = 12955.677150912 lb/d. What does the accepted topology guarantee about the mass?', '["That the mass closes, since a network with every well routed to a delivery point cannot lose any.", "Nothing. Validation settles what is legal to solve and never what the solve then does with the mass it was given.", "That any gap is smaller than the reported residual, which is the tightest number the return carries.", "That the gap belongs to the drawing, because a mass hole in a validated network can only come from a branch connected backwards."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gap is 345.000000000 lb/d, 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says was produced, on a topology that passed every one of the eleven checks.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 1, 'On the published ladder W-0 makes 3522.516744485 lb/d alone on the header and 3137.891322295 lb/d with two companions on it. What changed about W-0 between those two rows?', '["Its flowline conductance, which the ladder reduces at each rung so that the header can be shared between more wells.", "Its inflow curve, which is re-evaluated against the new header and comes back flatter once two more wells are drawing on the same trunk.", "Nothing about the well. Its reservoir, its inflow curve and its flowline are identical in both rows.", "Its allocation, which is divided among the wells on the header as each one is added."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The loss of 384.625422 lb/d, 10.919052 percent of itself, is entirely the doing of the two wells that joined it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 2, 'Across the published ladder total delivered rises from 3522.516744 lb/d to 9076.922229 lb/d, so two extra wells bought 5554.405485 lb/d where their own solo rates suggested 6173.781457. Where did the difference go?', '["Nowhere. It was not transferred to the new wells and it was not produced at all.", "To the two new wells, which took it from W-0 because they arrive at the header with more pressure behind them.", "Into the trunk, which stores it as the extra pressure needed to pass the larger mass through the same line.", "Into the conservation gap, which is what the difference between a solo rate and a system rate is called."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Adding wells to a gathering system is always worth something and never worth what the well test says.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 3, 'W-0''s wellhead sits at 886.881507360 psia alone, 968.085702646 psia with one companion and 1172.493407776 psia with two. Why can no single-well study produce that column?', '["A single-well study uses a different inflow relation, so it cannot reproduce a Vogel well being pushed up its own curve.", "The wellheads were solved rather than measured, and a single-well study accepts only measured wellhead pressures as its boundary.", "Nothing stops it. The same three numbers come out of three single-well runs at three separator pressures.", "A single-well study is run against a wellhead pressure somebody typed in, so the number that moved here is one of its inputs rather than one of its outputs."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing about W-0 changed across the ladder, and the number that did change was supplied by the other wells.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 4, 'The header on the published ladder goes from 253.813945361 psia with one well to 670.128002137 psia with three, a rise of 416.314057 psi, with nothing done to the trunk. What is the mechanism?', '["The separator was raised as each well was added to the header, and a fixed separator pressure carries that rise straight back up through the trunk to every wellhead on the system.", "A turbulent branch costs pressure faster than it gains mass, so the same trunk asked to pass much more needs disproportionately more pressure behind it.", "Each new well adds its own wellhead pressure to the header, since a junction takes the sum of what arrives at it.", "The trunk conductance falls as its mass rises, which is what a square root law does to a branch."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The trunk went from passing 3522.516744 lb/d to 9076.922229 lb/d, and every well on the system then flows against a boundary that got worse while the well stood still.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 5, 'A sweep on the published three well case walks the separator from 120 psia to 400 psia and every well rate falls, W-0 from 3183.657116 to 2962.927699 lb/d. What is unusual about this lever compared with adding or removing a well?', '["It moves the header without moving any wellhead, which is why the rates change but the inflow curves are never re-entered.", "It is the only lever a diagnosis will nominate, because the boundary is the branch every network has.", "It moves every well the same way at once, so no well is ever traded against another.", "It changes the total without changing any individual rate, since the separator sets what is delivered rather than what is produced."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Total delivered goes from 9207.229955 lb/d at 120 psia to 8579.087131 lb/d at 400 psia, and the header follows from 624.301508 psia to 837.839001 psia.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 6, 'Why can a single-well study not price a change in separator pressure, even though the separator pressure is one of the things it is told?', '["Because a single-well study is told a wellhead pressure and never a separator pressure at all.", "Because the separator pressure enters a single-well study only through the fluid properties it sets at the surface.", "Because the sweep moves the well rates by less than the band the golden case is held to.", "Because the movement it would have to predict is the header, from 624.301508 psia to 837.839001 psia, and that is a network result nothing about one well explains."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Handing a single-well run 400 psia instead of 120 psia does not reproduce a table in which W-0, W-1 and W-2 all move together.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 7, 'On the teaching network AGBADA-2 makes 6890.874160167 lb/d alone and 6004.874117054 lb/d on the system, and the four solo rates add to 15683.052292561 lb/d against a system producing 13300.677150912. What does the pair of columns require?', '["Two solves against the same boundary, one with the well alone on its line and one with every well present, because neither column can be reached from the other.", "One solve and a correction factor of 15.190761959 percent applied to each well in turn, since the network cost is shared out across the four of them in proportion to what they make.", "One solve and the well test, since the solo column is what a well test measures and the system column is what the solve returns.", "One solve at each of two separator pressures, which is what the difference between the columns measures."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The network costs the four wells 2382.375141650 lb/d, 15.190761959 percent, and every wellhead in the right hand column was raised by the other three wells.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 8, 'Ranked by percentage lost the teaching wells run AGBADA-6 at 24.1629, AGBADA-9 at 15.9513, AGBADA-2 at 12.8576 and AGBADA-12 at 0.0000 percent. Ranked by rate on the system they run AGBADA-2, AGBADA-9, AGBADA-6 and AGBADA-12. Which meeting uses which list?', '["Both lists rank the same wells in the same order once the capped well is set aside, so either serves.", "An allocation meeting reads the rate list, and the loss list is the one that says where the system is taking the production.", "The loss list is the allocation list, since an allocation is settled on what each well is entitled to rather than on what it makes.", "The rate list is the only defensible one, because a percentage lost is not a measurable quantity."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The well at the top of the loss list is third on the rate list, and the two lists answer different questions about the same solve.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 9, 'AGBADA-6 has a Vogel qmax of 3300 lb/d at a reservoir pressure of 1650 psia and loses 24.162893177 percent of itself to the system while AGBADA-2 loses 12.857585591 percent. Why does the smaller well lose the larger share?', '["The smaller well sits on the slacker flowline at 365 lb/d per root psi, and a slacker line passes the header rise through to the sand undamped.", "Percentage lost is computed against the solo rate, so the smaller the solo rate the larger the percentage for the same loss in lb/d.", "The smaller well is furthest from the separator, so it sees the trunk drop and the manifold drop where the larger well sees only one of them.", "A header that rises takes a fixed number of psi out of whatever margin a well had, and the well with the lowest reservoir pressure had the least margin to give."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Its wellhead rose 485.665999 psi, from 335.147329 psia alone to 820.813328 psia on the system, against 1650 psia of reservoir behind it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 10, 'AGBADA-12 shows 0.000000000 lb/d lost on the teaching network, and its wellhead rose 527.461813 psi, the largest rise of the four. How should that row be read?', '["As the one well the network does not cost anything, since its flowline is the shortest on the system.", "As an artefact of the loss column, which is computed from the solo run and cannot be formed for a well whose solo run did not converge.", "Its reported rate stopped depending on pressure once the allocation of 985 lb/d began to bind, so the zero means the number cannot move rather than that the well escaped.", "As evidence that the header rise reaches the manifold and stops there, so a well on a capped line is shielded from it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its flowline passes 640.000000000 lb/d against a reported 985.000000000 lb/d, and the difference of 345.000000000 lb/d is the gap the audit reports on the whole network.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 11, 'A report quotes the 15.190761959 percent the teaching network takes off its own wells as a property of the field. What is wrong with that?', '["It is a property of one solve, at one separator pressure, with one set of conductances, and moving any of the three moves it.", "It is a property of the four wells rather than of the field, since each of them contributes its own percentage of itself to the total the report quotes.", "It cannot be quoted at all, because a percentage formed by differencing two separate solves has no published golden value standing behind either half of it.", "The number is right but the sign is not, since the wells lose to the network rather than the network to the wells."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It came from 15683.052292561 lb/d of solo rates against a system producing 13300.677150912 lb/d, and every one of those numbers moves with the boundary.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 12, 'Take AGBADA-2 off the teaching system and the rest produces 8290.169036003 lb/d, a gain of 994.366002145 lb/d, against the 6004.874117054 lb/d it was reported to be making. What is the deferment?', '["6004.874117054 lb/d, the rate the well was making, since production that a survivor makes instead is production the field was always going to have.", "5010.508114909 lb/d, which is the reported rate less what the survivors picked up.", "994.366002145 lb/d, which is the only rate anywhere in the pair of solves that was not being produced before.", "8290.169036003 lb/d, the rate the field falls back to once the well is off."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taking a well off drops the mass through the shared header, the header falls, and every surviving well moves up its own inflow curve.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 13, 'AGBADA-9 is reported at 3992.446687538 lb/d and hands the survivors 767.079317902 lb/d. AGBADA-6 is reported at 2318.356346320 lb/d and hands them 469.025752507. What decides how much the survivors pick up?', '["The share of the trunk the departing well was using, which is what the survivors are free to take once it is gone.", "The number of wells left on the system, since the gain is divided among them and a larger group recovers more of it.", "The margin the survivors have left over the header, rather than anything about the well that left.", "The departing well''s own conductance, because a stiffer flowline releases more header when it is taken off."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing was done to the survivors in either case, and the gain is a well losing rate to a new neighbour run backwards.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 14, 'The shut-in sweep gives AGBADA-12 a true deferment of 796.017158960 lb/d, measured from the 985.000000000 lb/d the engine reports for it. Its flowline passes 640.000000000 lb/d. What does that do to the row?', '["It overstates it. Measured from what the flowline actually delivers the deferment is 451.017158960 lb/d, exactly 345.000000000 lb/d less.", "Nothing, since the reported rate is what the well produces at the sand face and the flowline figure is simply what is left of it once the line loss along the branch has been taken off.", "It understates it, because the survivors gained 188.982841040 lb/d against a well the network was never receiving in full.", "It leaves it undefined, since a deferment cannot be formed for a well whose rate is set by an allocation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That row is overstated twice over, once by ignoring the survivors'' gain and again by starting from a rate the network never delivered.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-wells-fight', 15, 'A development case is built by adding the well test rates of three new wells to the field''s current production. What does the ladder say about that?', '["It is sound as long as every one of the three tests was taken at the same separator pressure the field runs at, since that is the boundary the whole system is solved against.", "It is sound for new wells and wrong only for the wells already on the manifold, whose own tests were taken back when the header they share was carrying far less.", "It understates the case, because the header falls as more wells share the cost of the trunk.", "The three solo rates are a promise the system does not keep, and 9076.922229 lb/d is what the same three wells deliver together."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A well that has been on a manifold for a year is already paying this and its test includes it, and a brand new well''s test does not.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 1, 'A big dry well at 2700 stb/d oil, 300 stb/d water, 1600 Mscf/d gas on 30000 lb/d joins a small wet one at 200 stb/d oil, 800 stb/d water, 90 Mscf/d gas on 10000 lb/d. What does the trunk carry?', '["The mass sum of 40000.000000 lb/d with each component re-split in proportion to it, which is what a header does to two streams of unequal size.", "The larger well''s components, since the smaller stream is carried within them and is metered out again at the separator.", "The component sums with the gas converted to a mass equivalent first, because Mscf/d and stb/d do not add on one branch.", "Oil = 2900.000000 stb/d, water = 1100.000000 stb/d, gas = 1690.000000 Mscf/d and mass = 40000.000000 lb/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Component rates add and nothing else happens to them, so the propagation introduces no error of its own.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 2, 'The two published wells sit at water cuts of 10.000000 and 80.000000 percent and the trunk they share is at 27.500000 percent. Why is the plain average of 45.000000 percent so far out?', '["The average is taken over the wells rather than over the branches, and there are more branches than wells on any real trunk.", "The average gives a well arriving on 10000 lb/d the same vote as one arriving on 30000 lb/d.", "The trunk cut is computed after the split at the junction, so it carries only the share of each well that took this route rather than the whole of it.", "The two cuts are quoted at the wellhead and the trunk cut at the separator, and a cut rises as the pressure falls along a line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Averaging the ratios is wrong by 17.500000 percentage points, a factor of 1.636364 on the number a facility would be sized against.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 3, 'The four teaching wells sit at water cuts of 11.239495798, 54.951600894, 26.151665485 and 77.263969171 percent, and the trunk arrives at 33.262507244. The plain average is 42.401682837. Where did the nine points go?', '["Into the loop, where the crosslink carries part of the wettest stream back into the manifold and counts it twice.", "Into the trunk, which arrives at a lower pressure than any wellhead so more of its gas has come out of solution.", "Into the rates. The wettest well is the smallest on the system, and the arithmetic hands it only its share of 3455.000000000 stb/d of oil and 1722.000000000 stb/d of water.", "Into the split at the loop tee, which divides the water differently from the oil."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The error is 9.139175594 percentage points, and it is set by how unequal the rates carrying the ratios are.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 4, 'Hand the same teaching network well stream masses at twice their honest value and the separator oil comes back at 3455.000000000 stb/d against 3455.000000000 stb/d, a difference of 0.0000e+0 stb/d. What does that make a water cut useless for?', '["Detecting a mass error, because a component ratio does not move when the mass carrying it is wrong.", "Sizing water handling, since a cut that does not respond to the mass cannot be scaled up to a design rate.", "Comparing two wells, since cuts formed on different masses share no basis.", "Anything at all downstream of a junction, since the split by mass share has already used the mass the cut ignores."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The split at a junction is by mass share and the shares are unchanged, so the oil, water and gas the separator is told to expect never move.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 5, 'A well of 400 stb/d oil arriving on 1000 lb/d leaves a junction by two legs carrying 750 and 250 lb/d. What oil goes down each?', '["200.000000000 stb/d down each leg, since a junction with two ways out divides what arrives evenly between them.", "Whatever each leg''s own bore admits, because a split by pipe size is the only split a topology can settle before the pressures are known.", "The whole 400.000000000 stb/d down the leg carrying 750 lb/d, with the second leg taking only the water and the gas.", "300.000000000 stb/d and 100.000000000 stb/d, which add back to 400.000000000 stb/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mass share is the only split that conserves anything, and dividing by pipe size or by drawn priority leaves the two legs no longer adding to what arrived.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 6, 'The loop tee on AGBADA WEST receives 3992.446687538 lb/d carrying 1042.000000000 stb/d of oil and sends 3402.582062368 lb/d down the loop leg and 589.864625170 lb/d out along the crosslink. What oil goes each way?', '["1042.000000000 stb/d down the loop leg, with the crosslink taking only the mass it needs to balance the manifold.", "888.049556192 stb/d and 153.950443808 stb/d, in the same proportion as the two masses.", "Oil in proportion to the two conductances, 245 lb/d per root psi on the loop leg against 540 on the crosslink, since the split is a property of the pipes.", "Half of the 1042.000000000 stb/d down each leg, because a component divides evenly once both ways out of a junction are open."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each component splits by mass share and no ratio is carried across the junction, so the water of 314.482040532 and 54.517959468 stb/d adds back to 369.000000000 stb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 7, 'The crosslink is drawn from the north manifold into the loop tee and the solve returns it at -589.864625170 lb/d. What happens to the propagation if the drawn arrow is believed?', '["Nothing downstream changes, since the totals arriving at the separator are the sum of the well tests either way.", "The propagation stops with the recirculation refusal, because a stream sent against a solved direction closes a cycle.", "The loop tee becomes a junction with one way out, and its stream of 153.950443808 stb/d oil, 54.517959468 stb/d water and 238.312923093 Mscf/d gas runs into the tee instead of out of it.", "The oil splits correctly and only the mass is wrong, because a component share does not depend on the direction of the branch."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every total past the north manifold is then wrong by a stream nobody subtracted, at full size rather than as a small error.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 8, 'Propagated along the solved directions, the teaching network delivers oil = 3455.000000000 stb/d, water = 1722.000000000 stb/d and gas = 3536.000000000 Mscf/d, and the four well tests come to exactly those three totals. Why do the components close so cleanly?', '["Addition is the whole of what happened to them, so the components arriving are the components handed in whatever the solve did with the pressures.", "The solve reconciles the component rates against the branch masses before returning them, which is what the propagation step exists to do.", "The network has one delivery point, and a single sink forces every component to close on a validated topology.", "The wells were tested at the separator, so their components were measured on the arriving stream in the first place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mass is the quantity that does not close: the trunk stream carries 13300.677150912 lb/d where the solve says that branch passes 12955.677150912 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 9, 'Which quantity in a propagated stream result can argue with the solve?', '["The water cut, which the solve computes independently from the well inflows and the propagation recomputes from the rates.", "The gas rate, since it is the only component whose value depends on the pressure the solve returned for that branch.", "The mass, because every branch carries a stream mass beside the flow the solve found for the same branch.", "None of them, because the propagation reads the solved flows and can only agree with them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the north bypass the stream says 9898.095088544 lb/d where the solve says 9553.095088544 lb/d, a difference of 345 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 10, 'Why does propagateStreams return oil, water, gas and mass on every branch and never a water cut or a gas-oil ratio?', '["A ratio cannot be formed on a branch carrying a backflow, and the module has no way of knowing in advance which of its branches the solve will reverse.", "A ratio would have to be re-derived at every junction, which the split by mass share has no way to do.", "The module returns ratios only where a delivery point is attached, since a cut has no meaning on an intermediate branch.", "A ratio it computed is a ratio somebody quotes later without the rates that made it, so every ratio is left to the reader."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The teaching trunk cut of 33.262507244 percent is right for the rates handed in, and quoting it without them says nothing about what the network delivers.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 11, 'The solved flow directions on a network close a cycle. What does the module do?', '["It refuses to propagate, reports that the network is recirculating, and suggests looking for a branch connected backwards.", "It iterates around the loop until the recirculating mass settles to a fixed point, and then reports that converged split as the answer for those branches.", "It propagates anyway and warns, because a recirculating direction is a fact about the answer rather than an error in it.", "It reverses the offending branch and re-propagates along the corrected directions."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A gathering system does not recirculate, so it reports the drawing mistake and computes nothing further.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 12, 'The separator on the teaching network sits at 265 psia and takes whatever arrives. What does the module model about it?', '["Its pressure and its liquid handling capacity, which is what the arriving water rate of 1722.000000000 stb/d is checked against.", "Nothing beyond that. There is no separation, no carryover, no temperature, no slugging and no holdup anywhere in the module.", "The flash at its inlet, which is where the arriving gas of 3536.000000000 Mscf/d is separated from the liquid.", "Its pressure and the drop across its inlet valve, which the trunk branch relation carries."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Well, junction and sink are the whole vocabulary, and anything a real facility does that is not one of those three has to be written as a branch relation or left out.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 13, 'Why is every flow in this module a mass rate in lb/d rather than a surface volume?', '["Mass is what the Vogel inflow returns, so converting it would cost an extra call at every well node.", "A branch relation is quoted per unit of mass, and volumes would have to be converted at each end of every branch.", "The separator meters mass, so the boundary condition would otherwise be in different units from the rest of the network.", "Surface volumes do not add across pressures, so mixing two of them is a mistake that hides for a long time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The components are still carried in stb/d and Mscf/d, and they add because a stream carries its own rates rather than a ratio.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 14, 'The trunk stream on AGBADA WEST carries 13300.677150912 lb/d and the trunk the solve found passes 12955.677150912 lb/d. Where does the difference come from?', '["AGBADA-12 was handed in at its allocation of 985 lb/d while its flowline passes 640 lb/d, and the shortfall rides along every branch downstream of it.", "The crosslink, which runs backwards and is therefore counted once in each direction along the path to the separator.", "The loop, where the two routes to the separator each carry part of the same stream and overlap at the trunk.", "Rounding in the split by mass share, which is applied once per junction along the path."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The same 345 lb/d appears on the north bypass and on the trunk, and it is 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says was produced.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-streams', 15, 'A facility is sized for water handling on the plain average of the four teaching well cuts, 42.401682837 percent, while the arriving stream is at 33.262507244 percent. What has the design bought?', '["A correct design, since the average is the conservative reading of the two and a water handling facility is always sized on the higher cut it might be asked to take.", "An undersized design, because the arriving cut is the one taken at the lowest pressure on the network and will rise further.", "Water capacity for a stream that never arrives, because the average over weights the wettest and smallest well on the system.", "Nothing either way, since a cut is a ratio and the sizing depends on the arriving rates rather than on the ratio."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two numbers differ by 9.139175594 percentage points, and the wettest well arrives on 985 lb/d against 6004.874117054 lb/d from the largest.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 1, 'The biggest drop on the teaching network belongs to the AGBADA-2 flowline at 476.806785609 psi. What is that number?', '["The difference between the two solved node pressures at the ends of that branch, and nothing more.", "The pressure the branch relation says is needed to pass 6004.874117054 lb/d through that line, computed from its bore and its friction factor.", "The drawdown from the reservoir pressure to the wellhead, which is what a flowline drop is on a well branch.", "The largest correction the line search accepted anywhere on that branch during the solve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It runs from a wellhead at 1257.276513629 psia to a north manifold at 780.469728020 psia, and a drop is exactly as good as the pressures it was differenced from.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 2, 'A gate fixture puts a choked flowline at 150.000000 psi over 300.000000 lb/d against a trunk at 250.000000 psi over 40300.000000 lb/d. The biggest drop is the trunk. What is the bottleneck?', '["The trunk, since it has both the largest drop and the largest mass and therefore the largest product of the two.", "Neither, because with the biggest drop on the trunk the module declines to name a bottleneck at all.", "The choked leg, at an intensity of 5.000000e-1 against the trunk''s 6.203474e-3.", "The trunk, because a bottleneck is the branch that would deliver the most if it were opened up."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A trunk carrying everything is supposed to have the biggest drop, and pointing at it every time would be useless.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 3, 'What are the units of the bottleneck ranking, and what may be done with them?', '["Percent of the total pressure the network spends, so two intensities subtract into the saving a fix would buy.", "Pressure over mass, psi per lb/d, and only the order is used because the units are arbitrary.", "Pressure, psi, the same as the biggest drop, which is why the two rankings can be read side by side.", "Mass per unit pressure, lb/d per psi, which is a conductance and therefore comparable with the branch conductance itself."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the teaching network the bottleneck scores 1.018377e-1 and the next branch 7.940329e-2, and the difference between those two is not a number about anything.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 4, 'One solve of AGBADA WEST puts the intensity ranking on the AGBADA-9 flowline at 1.018377e-1 and hands the pressure ranking to a different branch entirely. What has happened?', '["The ranking disagreed with itself, which is what a solve reporting a pinned node does to a diagnosis.", "The bottleneck ranking excluded the AGBADA-2 flowline because it carries the most mass on the network.", "The two branches are within a band the ranking cannot resolve, so either name would serve.", "Two different questions were asked of one solve, and they named two different branches."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The AGBADA-9 flowline spends 406.581740456 psi on 3992.446687538 lb/d while the AGBADA-2 flowline spends more pressure on more mass.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 5, 'The AGBADA-12 flowline is third in the bottleneck ranking at 7.922896e-2, on 50.706533886 psi and 640.000000000 lb/d. What should be checked before calling it a restriction?', '["Why the mass is small. That well is held to an allocation of 985 lb/d on a line capped at 640 lb/d, so the line sits at its limit by instruction rather than by resistance.", "Whether the branch is a backflow, since a negative mass in the denominator lifts a branch up the ranking.", "Whether its drop is larger than the trunk''s, because a flowline above the trunk in the drop ranking is the definition of a restriction.", "Whether the ranking was taken on the same solve as the pressures, since a diagnosis run on a stale answer ranks the previous case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A small mass in the denominator lifts a branch whether or not the smallness is a pipe problem, and 50.706533886 psi is not much pressure to have spent.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 6, 'How do the two rankings inside diagnose differ from each other?', '["In their basis. The biggest drop ranks on the signed pressure difference and the bottleneck on the magnitude, which is why a backflow can win one and not the other.", "In their population. The bottleneck ranks only branches carrying mass above 1e-9 lb/d while the biggest drop ranks every branch, including a dead one.", "In their direction. The biggest drop is taken along the drawn sense and the bottleneck along the solved sense, so a reversed branch appears in one ranking twice.", "In their scope. The biggest drop covers flowlines and trunk lines while the bottleneck covers only the branches between junctions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both rank on magnitude, on the absolute pressure difference and on the absolute drop over the absolute mass, so a dead leg with a large difference across it can be the biggest drop and can never be the bottleneck.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 7, 'The crosslink appears in the teaching ranking with a drop of -1.193210823 psi and a mass of -589.864625170 lb/d. How is it ranked?', '["It is dropped from both rankings, since a branch with a negative drop has no place in a ranking of resistance.", "On the signed values, so it sinks below every branch running as drawn.", "On the magnitudes, so it scores 2.022855e-3 psi per lb/d and sits at the bottom of the intensity ranking.", "On the magnitude of the drop and the sign of the mass, which is what marks it as a backflow in the same pass."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both its numbers are signed from the drawn from end to the drawn to end, and the solve found the branch running the other way.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 8, 'diagnose returns the crosslink in its backflows list. What has it found?', '["A branch whose flow reversed part way through the iteration and settled the other way round, which is the history the backflow list is there to record.", "A branch the solve could not settle, since a flow reported negative is a flow the Newton step was still moving.", "A branch drawn backwards, which is a drawing error the reader is expected to correct and re-solve.", "A branch running against the way somebody drew it, which is a fact about the answer rather than a fault in the system."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The drawing was a guess made before anything was solved, and on this network the loop tee simply sits above the manifold at 781.662938843 against 780.469728020 psia.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 9, 'diagnose reports dead legs = none on the teaching network. What has it checked?', '["That no branch is isolated from a delivery point, which buildNetwork would have refused earlier anyway.", "That no branch sits at zero pressure difference across its two ends.", "That every branch carries something, a dead leg being a branch the solve found carrying no mass.", "That every branch carries mass in the direction it was drawn in, so a backflow and a dead leg are the same finding reported twice."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The smallest mass on the network is the 589.864625170 lb/d the crosslink carries, and every other branch carries more.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 10, 'A report says a diagnosis found the crosslink running backwards and asks what the module did about it.', '["It named the branch and nothing else. It will not reverse the drawing, re-label the ends or re-solve, and every later reading stays in the drawn sense.", "It reversed the branch and re-solved, which is why the flow is reported with a minus sign against the original drawing.", "It refused the answer, since a solved direction against a drawn one is one of the eleven conditions that come back with a reason.", "It warned and left the stream propagation to run in the drawn sense, which is where the downstream totals then go wrong."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing refuses and nothing warns, so a reader skimming magnitudes will not see the minus on 589.864625170 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 11, 'A separator sweep on the published three well case moves the total from 9207.229955 lb/d at 120 psia to 8579.087131 lb/d at 400 psia. Why would no diagnosis ever propose that lever?', '["Because the sweep changes the answer rather than the network, and a diagnosis reports only on the network it was given.", "Because the module has no compressor as a node kind, so the separator pressure is not a quantity a diagnosis can read at all.", "Because a fixed pressure has no drop to rank.", "A ranking can only name a branch, and the boundary is not a branch."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four things a diagnosis returns are a bottleneck, a biggest drop, a list of backflows and a list of dead legs, and every one of them is a label over branches.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 12, 'What would it take to answer the question of what the network costs AGBADA-6, which the lesson gives as 24.162893177 percent of itself?', '["One solve and the bottleneck ranking, which names for each well the branch that is taking the rate away from it and by how much.", "Two solves and a subtraction, one with the well alone against the same boundary and one with the whole system present.", "One solve and the branch drop on that well''s flowline, which is the pressure the network took from it.", "One solve and the well''s own inflow curve, evaluated at the header rather than at the wellhead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alone it makes 3057.021085629 lb/d and on the system 2318.356346320 lb/d, and no ranking over one solve contains the first of those numbers.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 13, 'The teaching diagnosis was computed from a solve reporting converged = true after 11 iterations at a residual of 1.546141e-11 lb/d. What does that make the rankings?', '["Readings of the iteration''s own answer, since both columns of the ranking came out of the loop that produced the pressures.", "Checked results, because a diagnosis re-derives the branch masses from the pressures rather than reading the solved flows.", "Independent of the solve, since a ranking uses only the order of its numbers and not their values.", "Unaffected by the answer''s mass, because a ranking of pressure differences does not use the flows at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The audit on that same answer reports produced = 13300.677150912 lb/d against delivered = 12955.677150912 lb/d, a gap of 345 lb/d, and no ranking in the return knows about it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 14, 'A line is reported as spending 476.806785609 psi. What can this module say about the cause?', '["It can attribute the drop to friction and elevation separately, which is the split the branch relation returns beside the flow.", "It can give the equivalent length of the fittings on the line, which is the part of the drop a pipe schedule accounts for.", "Nothing at all. The branch relation is a callback the caller supplies, so no drop here resolves into a length, a bore, a roughness or a friction factor.", "It can say how much of the drop is turbulent, since the branch conductance in lb/d per root psi carries that information."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The module models no hydraulics of its own, and the well inflow is a callback as well, so both ends of every branch belong to the caller.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-result', 15, 'A ranking names a bottleneck and a meeting asks what fixing it would be worth. What can the module return?', '["The saving in psi, taken as the difference between that branch''s intensity and the next one down the ranking.", "The mass the branch would carry at a stated new conductance, which is the number the intensity is built to be scaled by.", "The rate every well would gain, which follows from the branch drop the fix removes.", "No price at all, because it has no pump, no compressor and no choke as a node kind and no equipment between nodes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An intensity is not a severity, its units are arbitrary and two of them do not subtract, so the ranking ends at an order and a name.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'State the claim this course is built on.', '["The rate a well makes is set by its own inflow curve, and the network decides only how much of that rate reaches the separator.", "The rate a well makes belongs to the whole system solved at once, against one boundary pressure, with every other well pushing on the same header.", "The rate a well makes is a property of the well, and a network study refines it once the flowline losses along its own branch are known.", "The rate a well makes is decided by the trunk, which is the branch every well has to share."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A study that begins by typing in a wellhead pressure has assumed away the question a network asks, and on the teaching system that assumption is worth 2382.375141650 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'Why is the linear case the anchor of the whole course rather than one example among several?', '["It is the only answer that can be checked with no tolerance in it, because a matrix inverse that shares no code with Newton lands on the same pressures.", "It is the fastest case, converging in 2 iterations where a turbulent tree needs 6 and the teaching network needs 11.", "It is the case the independent referee is most accurate on, sweeping the star 19 times against 48 for a turbulent tree.", "It is the only case whose golden values were computed by an inverse rather than by an iteration."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Newton lands 0.0000e+0 psia from the closed form at the first wellhead and the audit reports a gap of 7.275958e-12 lb/d, and every answer after that one is an iterate.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'The engine and an independent bisection referee land on 936.962342064 and 936.962342067 psia at the same header, a difference of -3.9037e-9 psia. What is that evidence about?', '["The engine''s Jacobian, which is what the 6 Newton iterations against 48 sweeps demonstrates.", "The convergence criteria, since both methods had to meet their own before the comparison could be made.", "The gate bands, which is what any agreement inside 5 decimals establishes.", "The physics, because two methods sharing no numerical machinery landing together is a statement about the model rather than about either code."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The audit on the engine''s answer for that case reports a gap of 9.890573e-8 lb/d, and the two arguments are separate ones.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'What is the right order for a network study?', '["Solve first to see whether the case is feasible at all, then validate the topology against the drawing, then propagate the streams along it and diagnose what comes back.", "Build, propagate the well tests along the drawing to get the branch masses, then solve for the pressures those masses would need, and diagnose the result at the end.", "Build and read the refusals, solve, read the flag with the audit beside it, list the negative flows, propagate, and diagnose last.", "Build, solve, diagnose to find the branch worth acting on, and propagate only if a stream split is asked for."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most of what goes wrong in a network study is a step taken before the one it depends on, and the counts have to match the drawing before a single pressure is computed.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'Why are the signed branch flows read before anything is propagated?', '["Because the propagation refuses to run until the backflow list has been read and acknowledged by the caller, which is the one interlock the module puts between a solve and a split.", "A junction with two ways out becomes a junction with one if a drawn arrow is believed, and every total past it is then wrong at full size.", "Because the flows are returned in the drawn sense and the propagation needs them converted to the solved sense first.", "Because a negative flow is the module''s way of reporting a branch it could not settle."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the teaching network the crosslink comes back at -589.864625170 lb/d and carries 153.950443808 stb/d of oil out of the loop tee rather than into it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'How is a shut-in priced?', '["Take the well''s reported rate and reduce it by the network cost percentage the fight table gives for that well.", "Take the well''s solo rate against the same boundary, since that is what the field loses when the well stops.", "Take the difference between the well''s solo rate and its system rate, which is the part of it the network was never receiving.", "Re-solve the system without that well and use what the survivors gain, never the rate the well was reported to make."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Taking AGBADA-9 off the teaching system gains the survivors 767.079317902 lb/d against the 3992.446687538 lb/d it was reported to be making.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'How is the question of what the system costs a well answered?', '["Solve the well alone against the same boundary, solve it on the system, and subtract, because neither of those columns can be reached from the other.", "Read the drop on that well''s flowline, which is the pressure the network takes from it.", "Take its share of the network cost percentage in proportion to the rate it makes on the system.", "Compare its wellhead on the system with the separator pressure the system runs against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'AGBADA-6 makes 3057.021085629 lb/d alone and 2318.356346320 lb/d on the system, and nothing in one solve contains the first of those.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'A study lists a trunk at 720 lb/d per root psi beside a branch at 400 lb/d per psi and a header drop in psi against a rating in psia. What is wrong?', '["Nothing, as long as every conductance is quoted on the same branch law and the pressures are all absolute.", "The conductances are fine and the pressures are not, since a header drop should be quoted in psia like every other pressure in the result.", "The two conductances are different quantities that never compare, and an absolute pressure is not a difference.", "The pressures are fine and the conductances are not, since both should be converted to a mass per unit of drop before they are listed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mass rate is lb/d everywhere, absolute pressure is psia, a difference and a wall rating are psi, and a turbulent conductance never compares with a linear one.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'Which of these does the module hand back to the caller rather than model?', '["The mass balance at a junction, which the caller states as a constraint over its own branches and hands in with the topology it built.", "The boundary pressure at the delivery point, which the solve determines for itself from the mass that turns out to arrive there.", "The split at a loop, which the caller supplies as a fraction the solve then holds fixed.", "The pipe hydraulics and the well inflow, since a branch relation and an inflow are both callbacks the consumer supplies."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every pressure drop in a result is a drop the caller''s own model produced, and no drop can be resolved into a length or a bore by anything inside the module.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'What is printed beside a converged flag before an answer is quoted to anybody?', '["The iteration count and the tolerance that was asked for, which together say how hard the answer was won.", "The iteration count, the reported residual and the conservation audit run on that same answer.", "The golden case the network most resembles, so the reader knows which published band the answer sits inside.", "The pinned list and the warnings, which are the only fields in the return that were not computed by the iteration."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine reports 11 iterations at 1.546141e-11 lb/d on the teaching network while the audit reports a gap of 345 lb/d, 2.593852900 percent of what it says was produced.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'A gathering system has a compressor between two junctions. How is it carried?', '["As a branch relation, or not at all, because a node is a well, a junction or a sink and there is no equipment between nodes.", "As a node of its own kind, which the module accepts alongside a well and a junction and reports among its unknown pressures.", "As a delivery point holding a fixed suction pressure, which is the closest any of the three node kinds comes to a machine that adds pressure.", "As a negative conductance on the branch it sits in, which is how a pressure rise is written."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A node kind the module does not have is refused with a reason rather than approximated, and saying which of the two you did is part of the answer.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'Which number in a network result was checked by something other than the iteration that produced it?', '["The reported residual, which is measured against the tolerance the caller asked for and is therefore a test of the answer against something outside the loop.", "The delivered mass, which the sink checks against the sum of what arrives on every branch attached to it at its own fixed pressure.", "None of them, unless the caller runs the audit itself or the case is linear enough to set against a closed form.", "The well rates, which are re-evaluated on the final solved pressures once the loop has finished, and so come from outside the iteration."]'::jsonb, 2, 'checkConservation sits in the same file and solveNetwork never calls it, and on the teaching network it reports a gap of 345 lb/d under a flag that says converged.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'The teaching wells rank AGBADA-2, AGBADA-9, AGBADA-6, AGBADA-12 by rate and AGBADA-6, AGBADA-9, AGBADA-2, AGBADA-12 by percentage lost. What does the second ranking pay for?', '["The knowledge of which well to work over next, since the well losing the largest share of itself is the one with the most left to recover downhole.", "The knowledge of which well is closest to its own inflow limit, which is the rate ranking read the other way up and carries no new information.", "The knowledge of how much of the whole field''s production the trunk line is holding back behind it at the present header.", "The knowledge that the well giving up nearly a quarter of itself is a header decision rather than a well decision."]'::jsonb, 3, 'AGBADA-6 loses 24.162893177 percent against AGBADA-2 at 12.857585591, and no amount of downhole work on the weaker well recovers a rise in the header.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'A facility is being sized from the teaching network and a report offers a water cut of 42.401682837 percent. What should be used instead?', '["The arriving cut of 33.262507244 percent, which falls out of the rates rather than being averaged over the wells.", "The wettest well''s cut of 77.263969171 percent, since a facility is sized on the worst stream it can be asked to take.", "The average of the two, since the arriving cut moves with the split and the plain average does not.", "Neither, because a cut computed on a stream mass the solve disagrees with cannot be used for sizing at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two differ by 9.139175594 percentage points, and the average over weights the wettest well because it is also the smallest on the system.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'What does this course claim you can now do that a single-well study cannot?', '["Predict a well''s rate from its inflow curve and its flowline without being handed a wellhead pressure at all.", "Solve a whole system at once, read what the result says, and name which of its numbers no single-well method could have produced.", "Audit a converged answer from inside the solver, since the residual and the conservation gap are two readings of the same quantity.", "Price a fix to the branch a diagnosis names, since the intensity ranking scales into a saving."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The teaching system takes 2382.375141650 lb/d, 15.190761959 percent, off its own four wells, and no amount of single-well work produces that number.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'What has to be true of a network before solveLinearNetwork can invert it in one pass?', '["Every branch has to run the way it was drawn, since a matrix assembled from a drawing cannot carry a reversed sign on one of its entries.", "The network has to be a tree, because a loop puts two unknowns into one equation and there is then nothing left for the inverse to solve.", "Every branch has to be a resistor and every inflow a straight line, which is what collapses the system to a weighted graph Laplacian.", "Every node has to be indexed before the separator, so the boundary column falls at the end of the matrix."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published star has conductances of 80, 120 and 400 lb/d per psi and linear wells of 60000 lb/d at 900 psia and 40000 lb/d at 700 psia, and it needs no iteration at all.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'On the published star the solver''s own residual is 7.2760e-12 lb/d and the conservation audit on the same answer reports a gap of 7.275958e-12 lb/d. A reader concludes that the flag and the audit measure the same thing. Where does that go wrong?', '["They agree on this case and that is a result about this case, since one is a maximum over nodal imbalances and the other is what the wells were credited with less what came out.", "It does not go wrong. The two are the same quantity computed twice, which is why the module returns only one of them.", "The audit is the tighter of the two, so a reader may quote it as the residual and drop the flag.", "The residual is in lb/d and the gap is relative, so the agreement is a coincidence of scale."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the teaching network the same two numbers are 1.546141e-11 lb/d and 345.000000000 lb/d, and their ratio is 2.231362e+13.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'A reviewer ranks four solves by iteration count: 2 on the published star, 6 on the published tree, 11 on the teaching network and 4 on one teaching well alone on its line. Which is the best answer?', '["The 2 iteration run, since Newton is exact on a linear system and the fewest steps means the least accumulated error.", "The 4 iteration run, which met the same tolerance in the fewest steps of the three nonlinear cases on the list.", "The 11 iteration run, because more Newton steps means a residual driven further down before the loop stopped.", "The count does not order them. It describes the path the iteration took and not the answer it arrived at."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 4 iteration run is the one whose conservation gap is 3.450000e+2 lb/d, relative 3.502538e-1, and it reports a residual of exactly 0.0000e+0 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'A published fixture runs a weak well against a 20 psia separator through a very slack line and converges at a wellhead of 20.003996803 psia. What keeps the solve off the floor of 14.7 psia?', '["The branch relation, which the contract requires to be monotone decreasing and therefore cannot return a pressure below the floor.", "The line search, which halves the correction until the residual improves and tries thirty halvings before giving up.", "The Jacobian step of max(1e-3, |p| * 1e-5), which is sized so that no differenced column can propose a step past the floor.", "The initial guess, which the module places at the delivery point pressure so that no node starts below it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A full Newton step on a network with a nearly dead leg will happily drive a node below atmospheric, and the rate here is 19.984012790 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'linear_star is held against the closed form at a relative 1e-12 and against the independent referee to 6 decimals. Why are the two bands so different?', '["The closed form is computed at higher precision, so the tighter band records the arithmetic rather than the physics.", "The referee publishes the golden values, so its band has to admit the rounding used when they were written to file.", "One compares two exact routes to the same answer and the other compares an exact route with a bisection sweep that stops on its own criterion.", "The relative band applies to pressures and the decimal band to flows, which are held to different counts throughout the gate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Newton lands 0.0000e+0 psia from the closed form at the first wellhead and 1.8679e-10 psia from the referee at the same node.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'The referee sweeps the published cases 19, 48 and 42 times where the engine takes 2, 6 and 6 Newton steps. What does the difference in counts license a reader to say?', '["Nothing about accuracy. The two methods stop on different criteria, one on how far the pressures moved and one on a nodal mass imbalance.", "That the engine is more accurate, since a Jacobian carries information a sweep has to rediscover at every node.", "That the referee is the more accurate of the two, since it visits every node many times over while the engine visits each one only a handful of times before it stops.", "That the two agree, since neither ever reached its cap of 4000 sweeps."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither criterion is published in the golden files, so a case both methods agree on says nothing about how either decided to stop.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Opening the midpoint leg of the published loop from 60 to 800 lb/d per root psi raises delivered from 6536.862501 to 7659.969113 lb/d, and nothing was done to the wells. Why does the total move?', '["The second route is now large enough to carry mass the first route was refusing, which is mass the wells were already making.", "The midpoint pressure falls to 234.053568 psia, and a lower pressure at a junction draws more mass out of the branch above it.", "The delivered figure counts both legs, so opening one leg adds its flow to a total the other leg already carried.", "The header falls from 949.106108 psia to 476.212272 psia, so every well on it moves up its own inflow curve."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A loop leg is not a private pipe, and the direct leg falls from 4926.564514 lb/d to 2991.534324 lb/d without being touched.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'One published ladder sends its header from 253.813945361 psia up to 670.128002137 psia and one published loop sends its header from 949.106108 psia down to 476.212272 psia. What is the header in both stories?', '["The pressure at the delivery point, restated at the first junction upstream of it.", "The pressure every well on the system is flowing against, which moves whenever what the system has to pass or the route it passes through changes.", "The average of the wellhead pressures on the system, which is why it rises when a well is added and falls when a route is opened.", "A boundary condition, which is why the two sweeps move it in opposite directions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Adding two wells cost the ladder 416.314057 psi of header, and opening a loop leg gave the loop case back more than that.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Walking the crosslink conductance on the teaching network takes its drawn pressure difference from -33.398604 psi to -0.182218 psi while its mass rises from -346.749154 to -597.618496 lb/d. What is happening to the two junctions?', '["They are closing on each other, because a fatter branch between two nodes carries more mass on less pressure difference.", "The manifold is rising toward the loop tee, which is what eventually reverses a backflow once the branch is large enough.", "The branch is approaching the conductance at which it stops carrying anything, which is where the drawn difference goes to zero.", "The trunk is taking the difference instead, which is why it moves from 12949.424167 to 12955.836128 lb/d over the same sweep."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every row of that sweep is negative, so no size of pipe puts the branch back the way it was drawn.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'The crosslink runs backwards and its intensity of 2.022855e-3 psi per lb/d puts it at the bottom of the teaching bottleneck ranking. How did the ranking treat the reversal?', '["It excluded the branch from the intensity ranking and left it in the drop ranking, which is why it appears in only one of the two.", "It ranked it on the signed values, which is why a negative drop over a negative mass lands at the bottom of the list.", "It ignored it. Both rankings work on magnitudes, so the sign neither promotes nor demotes a branch.", "It moved it to the backflow list instead, since a branch can appear in only one of the four things a diagnosis returns."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its drop of -1.193210823 psi and its mass of -589.864625170 lb/d are both signed from the drawn from end to the drawn to end.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'Which of these is decided by the drawing rather than by the solve?', '["Which branches carry mass and which are dead, since a dead leg is a branch nobody connected to a delivery point.", "Which nodes exist, which branches connect them and how many pressures are unknown.", "Which way each branch runs, since the drawn from and to ends are what the flows are signed against.", "How a loop divides its mass, since the two routes are drawn with their own conductances."]'::jsonb, 1, 'buildNetwork returns 8 nodes, 8 branches, 7 unknown pressures and 1 delivery point on the teaching network and never computes a pressure.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Two studies disagree about which way the crosslink runs. One reads the drawn arrow and one reads the sign on the returned flow. What settles it?', '["The pressure at the two ends, which has to be read in the solved sense rather than the drawn one before the flows can be signed.", "The topology, since the branch runs from the junction carrying more branches toward the junction carrying fewer.", "Neither on its own, since the module reports the magnitude and leaves the direction to the reader.", "The solve, because the direction is an output and the drawing was a guess made before anything was solved."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flow comes back at -589.864625170 lb/d, so the branch carries that mass from the loop tee into the north manifold.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'W-1 makes 2242.495611803 lb/d with one companion on the header and 2007.745062176 lb/d with two, a loss of 10.468272 percent. What is that percentage of?', '["Of W-1 itself in the two well case, since the comparison is the same well on the same header before and after a third well joined it.", "Of the header rise between the two rows, converted to a rate through W-1''s inflow curve.", "Of the total the three wells deliver, which is the base every well''s share is taken against.", "Of W-1''s solo rate, which is what a loss percentage is always taken against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'W-0 tells the same story over a longer run, losing 384.625422 lb/d, which is 10.919052 percent of itself.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'A study wants to know what the third well on the published ladder is worth. Which number answers it?', '["The third well''s own rate of 3931.285844958 lb/d, which is what it contributes to the header.", "The third well''s own rate less the header rise of 416.314057 psi, converted into a rate through that well''s inflow curve.", "The rise in delivered, since the total went from 3522.516744 lb/d with one well to 9076.922229 lb/d with three.", "The difference between the three wells'' solo rates and the delivered total, which is what the network takes off them for sharing a trunk."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two extra wells bought 5554.405485 lb/d against the 6173.781457 lb/d their own solo rates suggested, and no single-well study produces either figure.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'A gathering system is studied one well at a time, each against the separator pressure the field runs at. Which quantity is then guaranteed wrong?', '["The inflow curve, which has to be re-tuned against the header once the well is sharing that header with its neighbours.", "The wellhead pressure each well is flowing against, because every other well on the system raises it.", "The separator pressure, which a network solve determines rather than accepts.", "The flowline conductance, since a line that ends up sharing a header with other wells carries more mass than the study assumed for it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'W-0 flows at a wellhead of 886.881507360 psia alone and 1172.493407776 psia with two companions, and nothing about W-0 changed.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'The teaching network costs its four wells 2382.375141650 lb/d, 15.190761959 percent. What would move that number without touching a single well?', '["Nothing, because the figure is the difference between two solves and both were run on the same wells.", "Only the wells, since the number is built from four solo rates and four system rates and nothing else enters it.", "Only the trunk, because that is the one branch every well on the system has to share and therefore the only branch the whole cost can be attributed to.", "The separator pressure or any conductance on the system, since the figure is a property of one solve rather than of the field."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four solo rates add to 15683.052292561 lb/d and the system produces 13300.677150912 lb/d, both against a separator at 265 psia.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'AGBADA-6 loses 24.162893177 percent of itself and AGBADA-2 loses 12.857585591 percent, and AGBADA-2 is the larger well by a wide margin. What decides the ranking?', '["The flowline conductance, since AGBADA-6 sits on 365 lb/d per root psi and passes the header rise straight to the sand.", "The distance to the separator, since AGBADA-6 reaches it through the manifold and the trunk while AGBADA-2 reaches it directly.", "The margin each well has over the header, and the well with the lowest reservoir pressure had the least of it to give.", "The size of the well, since a percentage taken against a smaller solo rate is larger for the same loss in lb/d."]'::jsonb, 2, 'AGBADA-6 has a Vogel qmax of 3300 lb/d at 1650 psia and its wellhead rose 485.665999 psi, while AGBADA-2 has 8100 lb/d at 2750 psia and its wellhead rose 364.386971 psi.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'Shutting AGBADA-6 off the teaching system leaves the rest making 11451.346557099 lb/d, a gain of 469.025752507 lb/d among the survivors. What is the field losing?', '["1849.330593813 lb/d, which is what the well was reported to make less what the survivors picked up when the header fell.", "2318.356346320 lb/d, the rate the well was making, since a shut-in removes exactly what the well was producing.", "3057.021085629 lb/d, the rate the well makes alone on its own line, which is what it would have made had the system not been in the way.", "469.025752507 lb/d, since that is the only rate in the pair of solves that changed hands."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The survivors'' gain is a well losing rate to a new neighbour run backwards, and quoting the reported rate as the deferment is the standard overstatement.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'A deferment report on the teaching network quotes AGBADA-12 at 796.017158960 lb/d. Two separate corrections apply to that row. What are they?', '["The survivors'' gain, and the 15.190761959 percent the network takes off the field as a whole.", "The survivors'' gain of 188.982841040 lb/d, and the fact that the row starts from a reported 985.000000000 lb/d while the flowline passes 640.000000000.", "The header rise of 527.461813 psi at that well, and the 2.593852900 percent the audit reports against what was produced.", "The allocation of 985 lb/d, and the fact that the well was solved against a separator at 265 psia rather than at its own wellhead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Measured from what the flowline delivers the deferment is 451.017158960 lb/d, exactly 345.000000000 lb/d less than the row quotes.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'A fifth well is tied into a live gathering system. What happens to the water cut arriving at the separator?', '["Only the new well''s own stream moves it, since the wells already on the system are untouched by a stream that joins downstream of their own flowlines.", "Nothing moves it, because a water cut is a ratio and a ratio does not change when the rates that carry it into the trunk change around it.", "Only the arriving mass moves, since the components arriving are the tested components and a cut is formed from those components alone.", "Both its own stream and every other well''s rate move it, since the header rises and the weights are the rates."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the published ladder adding two wells took the first from 3522.516744485 lb/d to 3137.891322295 lb/d, so the weights behind any rate weighted number move as well.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'The four teaching wells are tested at 1690, 605, 1042 and 118 stb/d of oil and the separator is told it receives 3455.000000000 stb/d. Which of those five numbers came out of the solve?', '["The arriving total, which the propagation recomputes from the branch masses the solve returned.", "All five, since the well tests are scaled to the solved well rates before they are propagated.", "None of them. The components are handed in and added, and only the mass on each branch came from the iteration.", "The three that pass through a junction, since a split by mass share uses the solved flows and a well test on its own flowline does not."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trunk stream carries 13300.677150912 lb/d where the solve says that branch passes 12955.677150912 lb/d, and the components close all the same.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'A junction receives 3992.446687538 lb/d of a stream and sends 3402.582062368 lb/d one way and 589.864625170 lb/d the other. What decides how the oil divides?', '["The two masses, since a split by mass share is the only division at a junction that leaves the components adding back to what arrived.", "The two conductances, since a branch that can carry more of the mass carries more of every component in it.", "The two downstream pressures, since the leg with the larger drop pulls the heavier components toward it.", "The water cut on each leg, which the propagation carries across the junction and applies to the arriving stream."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil comes out at 888.049556192 and 153.950443808 stb/d, and the water at 314.482040532 and 54.517959468 stb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'A separator is being sized from a network answer and someone offers to check the arriving mass by looking at whether the arriving water cut is plausible. What is wrong with that check?', '["A cut is only defined at a delivery point, and the module returns no ratio anywhere in a stream result.", "A cut does not move when the mass carrying it is wrong, so it can never detect a mass error.", "A cut computed at the separator has already been through a split by mass share, so it carries the mass error twice over.", "A cut is quoted at the arriving pressure and the mass at standard conditions, so the two are on different bases."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Doubling every well stream mass on the teaching network leaves the separator oil at 3455.000000000 stb/d against 3455.000000000 stb/d, a difference of 0.0000e+0 stb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'In one published diagnose fixture a flowline spends 600.000000 psi on 2000.000000 lb/d, another spends 100.000000 psi on 40000.000000 lb/d, and the trunk spends 200.000000 psi on 42000.000000 lb/d. What do the two rankings say?', '["The drop ranking names the trunk and the intensity ranking names the choked flowline, which is the point of having two.", "Both name the trunk, since it carries the most mass and spends the second largest drop doing it.", "Both name the choked flowline, at an intensity of 3.000000e-1 against 4.761905e-3 on the trunk.", "The drop ranking names the choked flowline and the intensity ranking declines, since a leg at 2000.000000 lb/d is below the mass floor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A second fixture moves the biggest drop onto the trunk at 250.000000 psi while the bottleneck stays on the choked leg at 5.000000e-1, which is the whole design of the function.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'A branch carries no mass at all and has a large pressure difference across it. Where can it appear?', '["In neither, since a branch carrying nothing is reported as a dead leg and is removed from both rankings.", "In the biggest drop ranking but never in the bottleneck ranking, because one ranks every branch and the other ranks only branches carrying mass above 1e-9 lb/d.", "In both, since both rankings run over every branch in the result and differ only in what they divide by.", "In the bottleneck ranking only, since a vanishing mass in the denominator sends its intensity to the top of the list."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two rankings differ in their population rather than in their basis, and both work on magnitudes.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'The AGBADA-12 flowline scores 7.922896e-2 psi per lb/d and sits third in the teaching bottleneck ranking on a drop of only 50.706533886 psi. What put it there?', '["A small mass in the denominator, 640.000000000 lb/d, which is what the line passes because the well is held to an allocation of 985 lb/d.", "A large drop for a flowline, since 50.706533886 psi is more than the AGBADA-6 flowline spends on more than three times the mass.", "A backflow, since the well is producing more than its line carries and the difference returns along the same branch.", "The pinning of its node, which removes the branch from the mass floor the ranking applies to everything else."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A small mass lifts a branch up the ranking whether or not the smallness is a pipe problem, and this line sits at its limit by instruction.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A report says a network''s worst branch is the AGBADA-9 flowline. What is missing from that sentence?', '["The units, since an intensity of 1.018377e-1 is meaningless without the psi per lb/d that goes with it.", "The mass on the branch, since a ranking of pressure differences cannot be read at all without the flow that each of the branches in it was carrying.", "The solve, since a diagnosis has to be quoted with the iteration count and residual it was computed from.", "Which question was asked, since the drop ranking and the intensity ranking name different branches on that solve."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The biggest drop on that answer is the AGBADA-2 flowline at 476.806785609 psi and the bottleneck is the AGBADA-9 flowline at 1.018377e-1.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'A meeting asks a diagnosis what a fix to the bottleneck would be worth. What comes back?', '["The mass the branch would carry at a stated new conductance, which is the quantity the intensity ranking is built to be scaled by.", "The drop the fix removes, which is the difference between that branch and the next one down the drop ranking on the same solve.", "Nothing priced, because the units of an intensity are arbitrary, only the order is used, and two of them do not subtract.", "The rate the wells would gain, computed by re-evaluating each inflow curve at the improved header."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The module has no pump, no compressor and no choke as a node kind and no equipment between nodes at all.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'The teaching solve reports converged = true at 1.546141e-11 lb/d, and its diagnosis names a bottleneck and a biggest drop. How much of that reading is independent of the iteration?', '["The drop ranking is independent, since a pressure difference is a subtraction the iteration never performed.", "None of it. The drops are differences of solved pressures and the masses beside them are the solved flows.", "The intensity ranking is independent, since dividing by the mass cancels whatever error the pressures carry.", "All of it, since a ranking uses only the order of its numbers and an order survives an error in their values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The audit on that same answer reports produced = 13300.677150912 lb/d against delivered = 12955.677150912 lb/d, and nothing in the diagnosis knows about it.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'A branch ranking names the AGBADA-9 flowline as the bottleneck, and the well giving up the largest share of itself is AGBADA-6, whose flowline scores 1.740181e-2 psi per lb/d. What does that pair of facts mean?', '["A ranking over branches does not name the well the network costs the most, because what a well loses is a comparison of two solves rather than a property of any branch.", "The ranking is wrong on this network, since the branch belonging to the worst affected well should sit at the top of it.", "AGBADA-6 is losing its rate on somebody else''s branch, which the ranking correctly attributes to the AGBADA-9 flowline.", "The two agree once the crosslink is taken out of the ranking, since it is the branch that reverses."]'::jsonb, 0, 'AGBADA-6 loses 24.162893177 percent of itself while its own flowline spends only 40.343600289 psi on 2318.356346320 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'A study takes the drawn crosslink arrow at face value and propagates the streams along it. Which numbers go wrong?', '["Only the crosslink''s own stream, since the branch is the only one whose direction was misread.", "Only the mass column, since the components add to the same totals whichever way the branch is read.", "Only the trunk, since it is the branch downstream of both junctions and the errors cancel at the loop tee.", "Everything past the north manifold, because a junction with two ways out has been read as one and a stream nobody subtracted rides on."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The stream on that branch is 153.950443808 stb/d of oil, 54.517959468 stb/d of water and 238.312923093 Mscf/d of gas, and it is an error at full size rather than a small one.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'Two linear branches of 200 and 300 lb/d per psi collapse to one of 120.000000000 lb/d per psi. Why can the same trick not be used on the published loop''s midpoint route of 300 and then 220 lb/d per root psi?', '["The two branches are not in series, since the midpoint node also carries the leg the loop is drawn around.", "The rule holds only where both branches carry the same mass, and on a loop each leg carries its own share.", "The reciprocal rule is a fact about resistances, and a square root law has no equivalent single conductance to offer.", "The rule holds but the collapsed number would be in lb/d per psi, so it could not be compared with the direct leg at 180."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The route is two pressure drops against the direct leg''s one, which is why the leg quoted at 180 lb/d per root psi still takes 50.362456 percent.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The arriving water cut on the teaching network is 33.262507244 percent. A planner asks whether that number survives a well being shut in.', '["It does not, because the cut is weighted by the rates and shutting a well in re-solves every rate on the system.", "It does, because a cut is a ratio and a ratio does not move when the masses behind it change.", "It does, because the components are handed in from well tests and a shut-in removes one test from the sum without changing the others.", "It cannot be answered, since the module returns no cut and the number was formed by the reader."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking AGBADA-2 off leaves the rest making 8290.169036003 lb/d, and every remaining well moved up its own curve to get there.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Which pair of numbers in a network result is the pair a reader has to print together?', '["The reported residual and the tolerance that was asked for, since neither of those two numbers means anything at all unless it is quoted against the other one.", "The converged flag with its residual, and the conservation gap computed on the same answer by a call the solver never makes.", "The iteration count and the cap it was run against, since a run near its cap is a run that nearly failed.", "The delivered mass and the separator pressure, since the boundary is what the delivery was solved against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the teaching network those are 1.546141e-11 lb/d and 345.000000000 lb/d, which is 2.593852900 percent of what the engine says was produced.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'A network study is handed a drawing with a manifold that reaches no delivery point, and the engineer wants the rest of the system solved anyway. What does the module do?', '["It builds and solves the reachable part, returning the stranded node among the pinned ones with a warning.", "It builds the network and lets the solve decide, since a stranded node simply carries no mass and falls out of the answer.", "It builds the network and refuses only at the propagation step, where the stranded branch closes a cycle.", "It refuses to build the network at all and names the node, because nothing sets that node''s pressure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Solving such a drawing would produce a confident answer about a system that does not exist, so every failure there is a refusal with a reason rather than a repair.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'solveNetwork comes back with ok = false. What has happened?', '["The iteration ran its cap of 200 without meeting its tolerance, which is the failure the flag exists to report.", "A node was pinned and left where it sat, since a node the solver stopped moving cannot be part of an answer it calls solved.", "The Jacobian was singular, so two or more nodes move together and their pressures are not separately determined.", "The conservation audit failed, which is the one check that can veto a returned answer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That message points at a branch connected differently from the way the drawing suggests, and the other conditions come back as warnings the caller has to read.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'What does the module say a network needs before it will solve anything?', '["At least one well, at least one delivery point, a pressure on that delivery point, and a route from every node to it.", "A tree topology with no loop in it, a delivery point carrying a pressure, and a branch relation that is differentiable at every pressure difference.", "At least one well, a delivery point with a pressure on it, and an initial pressure supplied by the caller for each of the unknown nodes.", "A delivery point, a separator pressure below every wellhead, and no branch drawn against the flow."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing else puts anything into a network, and the delivery point is the boundary the whole system is solved against.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Which of these does the module refuse rather than warn about?', '["A solve that stopped making progress before it met its tolerance.", "A set of solved flow directions that closes a cycle, which is reported as a recirculating network.", "A node left at the pressure it happened to be sitting at, because nothing anywhere in the network depended on it.", "A solve that ran its iteration cap without meeting its tolerance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A gathering system does not recirculate, and the refusal suggests looking for a branch connected backwards.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Which quantity is NOT decided by anything in a network solve?', '["The delivery point pressure, which the caller fixes and the sink holds while it takes whatever arrives.", "The flow direction on a branch, which the drawing fixes once and for all when the branch is entered with its from and to ends.", "The share of a loop each route carries, which follows from the two conductances.", "The rate a well makes, which its own inflow curve fixes once the reservoir pressure behind it is known."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The teaching network is solved against a separator at 265 psia, and every other number in the answer moves when that one does.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'An engineer reports the teaching network as delivering 13300.677150912 lb/d because that is the mass on the trunk stream. What should have been quoted?', '["13300.677150912 lb/d, which is right, since the stream mass is the sum of the four well rates the solve returned.", "12955.677150912 lb/d, the mass the solve found on the trunk branch, with the gap of 345.000000000 lb/d stated beside it.", "The average of the two figures, since one of them is the mass handed in and the other the mass the solve found, and neither is the whole story on its own.", "15683.052292561 lb/d, since the four wells are capable of that much and the network only decides where it goes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stream was handed the well test masses, one of which is an allocation of 985 lb/d on a line that passes 640 lb/d.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Which number in a network study could only have come from solving the whole system at once?', '["The pressure drop along a well''s own flowline, since forming it needs a solved pressure at both ends of that branch.", "The water cut arriving at the separator, since forming it needs every well''s test to be carried onto the same trunk.", "The bottleneck, since the ranking runs over every branch in the network at once.", "The rate a well makes on the system, since every wellhead in that column was raised by the other wells."]'::jsonb, 3, 'AGBADA-2 makes 6890.874160167 lb/d alone and 6004.874117054 lb/d on the system, and neither column can be reached from the other.'),
  ('network', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'A network result is quoted with its converged flag, its iteration count, its residual, its pinned list and its warnings. A reviewer asks how much of that describes the answer rather than the run.', '["Only the pinned list and the warnings say anything about the answer, and even those are facts the iteration recorded about itself.", "The residual describes the answer, since it is the size of the error left in the pressures when the loop stopped.", "The iteration count describes the answer, since a run that took more steps drove the residual further down.", "All five describe the answer, since every one of them is returned by the same call that returns the pressures."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing in that return is a check on the answer that was not computed by the same iteration that produced the answer.');
