-- ============================================================================
-- PD7 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Production Networks, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE SOLVE. `solveNetwork` is the only function in
-- either module that iterates, and this tier is everything that needs the
-- whole system at once and NOTHING that needs to look at how the iteration
-- reported itself. The loop, the fight, the split, the reading. A gathering
-- system has one answer and every well is in it, so the rate a well makes is
-- not a property of the well, and this is the tier where that stops being a
-- slogan and becomes six numbers a learner has to produce.
--
-- THE TIER OPENS ON THE ONLY CHECK IN THE COURSE WITH NO TOLERANCE IN IT.
-- Give the solver LINEAR branch resistances and the whole system collapses to
-- a weighted graph Laplacian whose solution is a matrix inverse. Newton
-- iteration and Gaussian elimination share no code and no reasoning, so
-- agreement to machine precision is evidence about the assembly, the signs and
-- the boundary handling rather than about either method. On the published
-- linear_star the two agree EXACTLY at two of the four nodes and to the last
-- bits at the others, 5.7e-14 psia at the worst, and Newton needs two
-- iterations, because Newton is exact on a linear system and needing many
-- steps here would mean the Jacobian is wrong rather than that the problem is
-- hard. The gate asserts three iterations or fewer for exactly that reason.
-- EVERY RESULT AFTER THIS ONE IN THE WHOLE COURSE IS AN ITERATE, and a tier
-- that did not open here would never have shown a learner the difference.
--
-- THE RESULT THE MODULE EXISTS FOR, AND THE RANKING THAT REVERSES. WELLS
-- FIGHT, AND THE WEAK WELL LOSES MOST. On the teaching network the four solo
-- rates add to 15683.052293 lb/d and the system produces 13300.677151, so the
-- network costs 2382.375142 lb/d, 15.190762 percent. The ranking by percentage
-- lost is NOT the ranking by rate: the low pressure well loses 24.162893
-- percent of itself while the strongest well loses 12.857586, and the ranking
-- by rate on the system puts them the other way round. A well with little
-- margin over the header has the least to give when the header rises. Neither
-- column can be got at from the other by any single-well method, because every
-- single-well study is run against a wellhead pressure somebody typed in, and
-- the published wells_fight ladder shows the same thing built up one well at a
-- time on a case a lesson may quote.
--
-- AND THE SHUT-IN SWEEP IS IN THE SAME SECTION FOR A REASON. Taking a well off
-- the system, the survivors' gain is nothing like the rate the shut well was
-- reported to be making: 994.366002 lb/d of gain against 6004.874117 lb/d
-- reported, on the strongest well. Reading the reported rate as the deferment
-- is the standard way a deferment number is overstated, and it is the mistake
-- a network answer is FOR preventing.
--
-- THREE THINGS THE TIER MUST NOT LET A LEARNER ASSUME.
--
--   A DRAWN ARROW IS NOT A DIRECTION, AND THE SIGN IS A PROPERTY OF THE
--   SOLUTION. The teaching crosslink is drawn from the north manifold to the
--   loop tee and the solve returns MINUS 589.864625 lb/d, so it carries mass
--   the other way, and the pressure across it in the drawn sense is minus
--   1.193211 psi. The loop tee is fed by one well through a stiff flowline and
--   the manifold by three through slacker ones, so at the solution the drawn
--   downstream end is the HIGHER of the two. The drawing was a guess made
--   before anybody solved anything. `diagnose` names it as a backflow, and
--   that name is the only thing in the whole return that does.
--
--   THE BOTTLENECK IS NOT THE BIGGEST DROP. The bottleneck is the branch
--   eating the most pressure per unit of what it carries, because a trunk line
--   carrying everything is SUPPOSED to have the biggest drop and pointing at
--   it every time would be useless. The two are different branches on every
--   real case in the digest, and a lesson that treated them as one word would
--   be teaching a reader to recommend replacing the one line on the system
--   that is doing its job.
--
--   AND STREAMS ADD BY RATE AND NEVER BY RATIO. A node with more than one way
--   out splits its stream by MASS SHARE, which is the only split that
--   conserves anything, and the component rates that ride along are exact
--   arithmetic rather than an iterate. Averaging two water cuts at a junction
--   is the mistake this module's currency rule exists to prevent, and it is
--   why every flow in the course is a mass.
--
-- WHAT THIS TIER MUST NOT PROMISE, AND IT IS HANDED STRAIGHT UPWARD. A
-- CONVERGED FLAG IS NOT A RESULT. Everything above is read off a return whose
-- own quality number was computed by the same iteration that produced the
-- answer, and the independent check that would test it sits in the same file
-- and is never called. This tier states that as a limit rather than as a
-- suspicion, because it can be stated honestly without any of the Expert
-- material: nothing in the return of `solveNetwork` is a check on the answer
-- that was not computed by the same iteration that produced it. What that is
-- worth in pounds a day is the tier above.
--
-- The only numbers that travel to the tier above are the system's own stated
-- conditions. The Expert tier restates all of them and rebuilds every
-- coefficient it uses.
-- ============================================================================

values ('network', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-the-solve","title":"The Solve","lesson_keys":["l01-the-one-case-with-a-closed-form","l02-newton-on-a-nodal-mass-balance","l03-the-jacobian-and-the-line-search","l04-a-tree-of-turbulent-branches","l05-what-convergence-is-measured-on"]},{"key":"m02-loops-and-directions","title":"Loops and Directions","lesson_keys":["l01-a-loop-is-what-makes-a-network","l02-the-split-is-a-decision","l03-walking-a-leg-and-watching-the-share","l04-a-drawn-arrow-is-not-a-direction","l05-what-a-topology-cannot-decide"]},{"key":"m03-wells-fight","title":"Wells Fight","lesson_keys":["l01-one-well-then-two-then-three","l02-the-header-rises","l03-what-the-network-costs-each-well","l04-the-weak-well-loses-most","l05-what-a-deferment-number-overstates"]},{"key":"m04-streams","title":"Streams","lesson_keys":["l01-component-rates-add","l02-never-average-a-ratio","l03-a-split-by-mass-share","l04-what-arrives-at-the-separator"]},{"key":"m05-reading-the-result","title":"Reading the Result","lesson_keys":["l01-the-biggest-drop","l02-the-bottleneck","l03-backflows-and-dead-legs","l04-what-a-diagnosis-does-not-decide"]},{"key":"m06-the-professional-reading","title":"The Professional Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
